<section xml:id="topic-1-6-variables">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Variables</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <remark>
      <title>1.6.A</title>
      <p>Identify the use of variables in an app while interacting with it.</p>
    </remark>

    <interactive iframe="webapps/pizzaApp.html" width="100%" aspect="2.5:1.2" />

    <remark>
      <title>1.6.B</title>
      <p>Develop code for assignment statements using variables, and determine the values that are
        stored in the variables as a result.</p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>1.6.C</title>
      <p>Develop code to store and modify strings, and determine the output produced.</p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Identifying Variables in Apps</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.1</title>
      <p>A variable is a named storage location used to hold a value that can change while the
        program is running. It is an abstraction that lets programmers use a name to represent a
        value.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.2</title>
      <p>Apps use variables to keep track of changing information by updating their values as the
        user interacts with the app. Programs can then use a variable’s current value to decide what
        to do next and what feedback to provide, such as updating the screen, playing a sound, or
        triggering a vibration.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.3</title>
      <p>Every value in a program has a data type, such as a number, Boolean, or string. Some
        programming languages require programmers to specify the type, while others determine it
        automatically by how the data is used.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.4</title>
      <p>The choice of data type depends on what a value represents. Values used to count or measure
        are best represented as numbers, values that record a true or false condition are best
        represented as Booleans, and values that store text are best represented as strings. The
        data type affects what operations can be performed on the variable and how accurately the
        output is represented in an app.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.5</title>
      <p>Variables are often used to store information about the user or a program’s state, such as
        a username, a selected theme, a score, progress toward a goal, a shopping cart total, or
        whether notifications are on. The program can use the stored value to adjust its behavior or
        display the value to the user.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.6</title>
      <p>Program state is the set of values and information a program is storing at a specific
        moment, including variable values and any inputs it is using. As the program runs, the state
        changes when inputs are received and variables are updated.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.7</title>
      <p>Users can infer the use of variables in an app by observing information that changes over
        time, such as counters, totals, settings, progress, or status, and reasoning about what
        values the app must store and update to produce those changes.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Variables and Assignment</title>
    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.1</title>
      <p>The assignment operator allows a program to initialize or change the value stored in a
        variable. When the following code runs, <c>expression</c> is evaluated, and the resulting
        value is stored in the variable <c>a</c>. <c>a = expression</c></p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.2</title>
      <p>Variables are initialized by assigning an initial value before they are used. After
        initialization, later statements can use the variable’s value and update it as the program
        runs.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.3</title>
      <p>Using meaningful variable names improves readability and helps programmers understand what
        the stored values represent.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.4</title>
      <p>A variable stores its most recently assigned value.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.5</title>
      <p>The value stored in a variable after a sequence of assignment statements can be determined
        by tracing statements in order and updating the variable’s value after each assignment.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.6</title>
      <p>Assignment statements are often used to update information an app remembers about the user
        or the program’s state, such as a score or setting, so the app responds appropriately as the
        user interacts with it.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Strings</title>
    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.1</title>
      <p>A string is an ordered sequence of characters used to represent text in a program.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.2</title>
      <p>Apps often use strings to store and display user-facing information, such as names,
        messages, labels, or search terms.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.3</title>
      <p>To initialize a string variable, programmers assign it an initial text value or an empty
        string before it is used so the program starts with a known value. An empty string has zero
        characters (<c>""</c>). It is not the same as a string that contains spaces (<c>" "</c>).</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.4</title>
      <p>String concatenation joins two or more strings to create a new string, such as combining a
        greeting with a user’s name.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.5</title>
      <p>A substring is part of an existing string. Programs can create substrings to extract or use
        part of a text value, such as extracting the month from a date, reading a portion of a
        message, or checking for a word within a string.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.6</title>
      <p>The output of code that uses string operations can be determined by tracing statements in
        order and keeping track of how string values change and what text is displayed.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.7</title>
      <p>To communicate clearly with users, programmers use strings to store and generate text that
        apps display, such as prompts, feedback messages, and personalized responses.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
</section>